<BaseLayout title="About" description="About Yan Zeng and current academic interests." pageKey="about">
  <div class="page">
    <header class="page-title">
      <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
      <h1>About Yan Zeng</h1>
      <p>
        I study how AI systems reshape trust, responsibility, and human agency.
      </p>
    </header>

    <article class="prose">
      <p>
        Yan Zeng is a PhD student in philosophy at the University of California,
        Irvine. His work lies at the intersection of epistemology, AI ethics,
        and philosophy of technology.
      </p>
      <p>
        His current research asks how trust, dependence, and responsibility are
        structured in human-AI relations. A central theme is that dominant
        approaches to AI trust often focus too narrowly on system properties
        such as transparency, reliability, robustness, fairness, and
        explainability. Against this property-based paradigm, his work develops
        a structural account of AI trust that asks whether human participants
        occupy fair and practically inhabitable roles within relations of
        dependence.
      </p>
      <p>
        He also works on collective forgiveness, collective agency, and the
        practical-normative structure of interpersonal and political repair. More
        broadly, he is interested in virtue epistemology, skepticism,
        experimental philosophy, and philosophical methodology.
      </p>

      <h2>Research Areas</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Epistemology, virtue epistemology, and social epistemology.</li>
        <li>AI ethics, trust, reliance, and human-AI dependence.</li>
        <li>Philosophy of technology and ethics of automation.</li>
        <li>Collective responsibility, collective forgiveness, and repair.</li>
        <li>Experimental philosophy and the methodology of philosophical intuitions.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Public Writing</h2>
      <p>
        The writing here is intentionally selective. It is meant to turn ongoing
        philosophical work into durable public notes while keeping drafts,
        private records, and raw conversations out of the published site.
      </p>

      <h2>About This Site</h2>
      <p>
        This site is both an academic profile and a public notebook. It collects
        reviewed research notes, essays, projects, creative experiments, and
        selected reflections that are suitable for public circulation.
      </p>
      <p>
        Unpublished manuscripts, private drafts, raw conversations, and
        intellectual work that is still under review are not posted here. Public
        writing is treated as a form of responsibility, not automatic disclosure.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
